  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Spades (Standard). Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Spades is a trick-taking card game devised in the United States in the 1930s. It is played by four players in partnerships, using a standard 52-card deck. Spades are always the trump suit. The object is to score points by winning at least the number of tricks bid in each hand. Bids are made before play begins. 'Bags' (overtricks) and 'Nil' bids add strategic depth.",
    objective: 'To score points, generally 500. Points are accrued by winning at least the number of tricks bid in each hand; they are lost by failing to take at least that many, or in some cases by taking too many (bags).',
    gameplay: "Deal 13 cards each. Each player bids 0–13 tricks (Nil = 0). Player to dealer's left leads first; may not lead a spade until spades are broken or holder has only spades. Must follow suit if able; otherwise may play any card. Trick won by highest spade if any, else highest of led suit. Winner leads next. Thirteen tricks per hand. Score 10 per bid trick if made; 1 per overtrick (bag); 10 bags = -100. Nil: +100 or -100; blind nil: ±200. First to 500 wins.",
    keyRules: [
      'Spades are always trump. Must follow suit if able. Spades may not be led until broken or only spades in hand. Partnership bids are summed; team must make combined bid. Overtricks (bags) score 1 each; 10 bags cost 100 points.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: true,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
